(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_unitsandmeasurements=[{id:"neet-2026r-q002",pn:2,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Vernier calliper zero error",q:"One main scale division of a Vernier calliper is equal to 1 mm and the number of divisions on the Vernier scale is 10. When both the jaws touch each other, the Vernier scale shifts to the left of zero of the main scale in such a way that 4th Vernier division coincides with a division of the main scale. If this Vernier calliper measures the length of the wire to be 1 cm, the actual length of the wire is :",o:["0.96 cm","1.00 cm","1.04 cm","0.60 cm"],a:"No option is correct",s:`The Vernier scale is to the left of zero, so there is a negative zero error.
Zero error = \u2013(10 \u2013 4) \xD7 L.C. (L.C. = 0.01 cm).
Zero error = \u2013(10 \u2013 4) \xD7 0.01 = \u20130.06 cm.
Zero correction = \u2013 Zero error = \u2013(\u20130.06) = 0.06 cm.
Correct reading R\u2032 = R + zero correction = 1 + 0.06 = 1.06 cm.
No option gives 1.06 cm, so no option is correct.
Note: Printed answer: No option is correct (working gives 1.06 cm)`},{id:"neet-2026-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Changing system of units",q:"The speed of light in vacuum is taken as unity. If light takes 6 min 40 s to reach the Earth from the Sun, the distance between the Sun and the Earth in new unit is :",o:["3 \xD7 10\u2078","3 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070","400","500"],c:2,s:`Time t = 6 min 40 s = 360 + 40 = 400 s.
Distance d = vt.
With v = 1 in the new system, d = 1 \xD7 400 = 400.`},{id:"neet-2026-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Vernier callipers least count",q:"In a vernier callipers, 20 VSD coincide with 16 MSD (each division of length 1 mm). The least count of the vernier callipers is-",o:["0.01 cm","0.1 cm","0.02 cm","0.2 cm"],c:2,s:`Least count L.C. = 1 MSD \u2212 1 VSD ... (i)
20 VSD = 16 MSD, so 1 VSD = (16)/(20) MSD.
From (i): L.C. = 1 MSD \u2212 (16)/(20) MSD = (4)/(20) MSD.
L.C. = (1)/(5) mm = 0.2 mm.
L.C. = 0.02 cm.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q009",pn:9,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions of constants",q:"Consider that \u03C3\u209B, k_B, b represents Stefan-Boltzmann constant, Boltzmann constant and Wien\u2019s displacement law constant, respectively. The dimension of \u03C3\u209B k_B\u207B\xB9 b is",o:["[L\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2]","[L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB3K\u207B\xB3]","[L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\u2074]","[L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2]"],c:3,s:`\u03C3\u209B = MT\u207B\xB3K\u207B\u2074.
k_B = M\xB9L\xB2T\u207B\xB2K\u207B\xB9.
b = M\u2070L\xB9T\u2070K\xB9.
\u03C3\u209B k_B\u207B\xB9 b = [MT\u207B\xB3K\u207B\u2074][M\u207B\xB9L\u207B\xB2T\xB2K][M\u2070LT\u2070K].
= T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2L\u207B\xB9.
= L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2026-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Significant figures",q:"Each side of a metallic cube of mass 5.580 kg is measured to be 9.0 cm. Keeping the significant figures in view, the density of the material of the cube can be best expressed as X \xD7 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3 where the value of X is:",o:["7.654","7.7","7.65","7.6"],c:1,s:`Density = Mass/Volume = 5.580 kg/(9.0 cm)\xB3.
= (5.580/729) \xD7 10\u2076 kg/m\xB3.
= 0.007654321 \xD7 10\u2076 kg/m\xB3.
= 7.654 \xD7 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3.
The side 9.0 cm has only 2 significant figures, so the answer is 7.7 \xD7 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3.
Hence X = 7.7.`},{id:"neet-2026-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Simple pendulum experiment",q:`Savitha, a XI standard student, while conducting an experiment to determine the effective length of a simple pendulum L, notes down the data of time taken to complete 30 oscillations as 60 s and hence calculates the length of the simple pendulum as:
(Take \u03C0\xB2 = 9.8, and g = 9.8 m/s\xB2)`,o:["2 m","0.75 m","1.5 m","1 m"],c:3,s:`Time for 30 oscillations = 60 s.
Time period = time for 1 oscillation, so T = 60/30 = 2 s.
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(\u2113/g) gives \u2113 = gT\xB2/4\u03C0\xB2.
\u2113 = (9.8 \xD7 2\xB2)/(4 \xD7 9.8) = 1 m.`},{id:"neet-2025-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional analysis",q:"A balloon is made of a material of surface tension S and its inflation outlet (from where gas is filled in it) has small area A. It is filled with a gas of density \u03C1 and takes a spherical shape of radius R. When the gas is allowed to flow freely out of it, its radius r changes from R to 0 (zero) in time T. If the speed v(r) of gas coming out of the balloon depends on r as r\u1D43 and T \u221D S\u1D45 A\u1D5D \u03C1\u03B3 R\u03B4 then",o:["a = 1/2, \u03B1 = 1/2, \u03B2 = \u22121, \u03B3 = +1, \u03B4 = 3/2","a = \u22121/2, \u03B1 = \u22121/2, \u03B2 = \u22121, \u03B3 = \u22121/2, \u03B4 = 5/2","a = \u22121/2, \u03B1 = \u22121/2, \u03B2 = \u22121, \u03B3 = 1/2, \u03B4 = 7/2","a = 1/2, \u03B1 = 1/2, \u03B2 = \u22121/2, \u03B3 = 1/2, \u03B4 = 7/2"],c:2,s:`T \u221D S\u1D45A\u1D5D\u03C1\u03B3R\u03B4
M\u2070L\u2070T\xB9 = K (MT\u207B\xB2)\u1D45 (L\xB2)\u1D5D (ML\u207B\xB3)\u1D5E L\u03B4
M\u2070L\u2070T\xB9 = K [M^(\u03B1+\u03B3) L^(2\u03B2\u22123\u03B3+\u03B4) T^(\u22122\u03B1)]
\u22122\u03B1 = 1, so \u03B1 = \u22121/2
\u03B1 + \u03B3 = 0, so \u03B3 = 1/2
2\u03B2 \u2212 3\u03B3 + \u03B4 = 0
2\u03B2 \u2212 3(1/2) + \u03B4 = 0
By hit and trial (using option (1)), put \u03B2 = \u22121:
2(\u22121) \u2212 3/2 + \u03B4 = 0, so \u03B4 = 7/2
Note: the solution says 'using option (1)' for the trial of \u03B2 = \u22121, but the matching printed option is (3); the exponent 'a' of v(r) is not worked out in the solution`},{id:"neet-2025-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Vernier callipers, zero error",q:`Consider the diameter of a spherical object being measured with the help of a Vernier callipers. Suppose its 10 Vernier Scale Divisions (V.S.D.) are equal to its 9 Main Scale Divisions (M.S.D.). The least division in the M.S. is 0.1 cm and the zero of V.S. is at x = 0.1 cm when the jaws of Vernier callipers are closed.
If the main scale reading for the diameter is M = 5 cm and the number of coinciding vernier division is 8, the measured diameter after zero error correction, is`,o:["5.18 cm","5.08 cm","4.98 cm","5.00 cm"],c:2,s:`Least count = 1 MSD \u2212 1 VSD = 1 MSD \u2212 (9/10) MSD = (1/10) MSD
Least count = (1/10) \xD7 0.1 cm = 0.01 cm
Zero error = +0.1 cm
Main scale reading = 5 cm
Vernier scale reading = 8 \xD7 0.01 = 0.08 cm
Final diameter = 5 + 0.08 \u2212 0.1 = 4.98 cm`},{id:"neet-2025-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Percentage error",q:`A physical quantity P is related to four observations a, b, c and d as follows:
P = a\xB3b\xB2 / c\u221Ad
The percentage errors of measurement in a, b, c and d are 1%, 3%, 2%, and 4% respectively. The percentage error in the quantity P is`,o:["10%","2%","13%","15%"],c:2,s:`Maximum % error in P = (\u0394P/P) \xD7 100 = 3(\u0394a/a \xD7 100) + 2(\u0394b/b \xD7 100) + (\u0394c/c \xD7 100) + (1/2)(\u0394d/d \xD7 100).
= 3 \xD7 (1) + 2 \xD7 (3) + (2) + (1/2) \xD7 (4)
= 13%`},{id:"neet-2024-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Vernier constant",q:"In a vernier calipers, (N + 1) divisions of vernier scale coincide with N divisions of main scale. If 1 MSD represent 0.1 mm, the vernier constant (in cm) is-",o:["100 N","10(N + 1)","1/(10 N)","1/(100(N + 1))"],c:3,s:`Vernier constant V.C = MSD \u2013 VSD   ...(1)
Given (N + 1) VSD = N MSD, so VSD = (N/(N + 1)) MSD   ...(2)
From (1) and (2): V.C = MSD \u2013 (N/(N + 1)) MSD = MSD (1 \u2013 N/(N + 1)) = MSD/(N + 1).
1 MSD = 0.1 mm = 0.01 cm.
So V.C = 0.01/(N + 1) = 1/(100(N + 1)) cm.`},{id:"neet-2024-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions of dimensionless quantities",q:"The quantities which have the same dimensions as those of solid angle are;",o:["strain and arc","angular speed and stress","strain and angle","stress and angle"],c:2,s:`Solid angle d\u03A9 = dA/r\xB2 has dimensions [M\u2070L\u2070T\u2070].
Strain = \u0394\u2113/\u2113 has dimensions [M\u2070L\u2070T\u2070].
An angle in radians, \u03B8 = \u2113/r, is also dimensionless [M\u2070L\u2070T\u2070].`},{id:"neet-2024-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional analysis",q:"A force defined by F = \u03B1t\xB2 + \u03B2t acts on a particle at a given time t. The factor which is dimensionless, if \u03B1 and \u03B2 are constants, is:",o:["\u03B1\u03B2t","\u03B1\u03B2/t","\u03B2t/\u03B1","\u03B1t/\u03B2"],c:3,s:`By the principle of homogeneity, [F] = [\u03B1t\xB2] = [\u03B2t].
So [\u03B1] = [F]/[t\xB2] and [\u03B2] = [F]/[t].
Then [\u03B1][t] = [\u03B2].
Therefore \u03B1t/\u03B2 is dimensionless.`},{id:"neet-2023-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Error propagation",q:"A metal wire has mass (0.4 \xB1 0.002) g, radius (0.3 \xB1 0.001) mm and length (5 \xB1 0.02) cm. The maximum possible percentage error in the measurement of density will nearly be:",o:["1.4%","1.2%","1.3%","1.6%"],c:3,s:`Density \u03C1 = M/(\u03C0r\xB2\u2113).
Maximum fractional error: \u0394\u03C1/\u03C1 = \u0394M/M + 2\u0394r/r + \u0394\u2113/\u2113.
= 0.002/0.4 + 2 \xD7 0.001/0.3 + 0.02/5.
= 0.0156.
Percentage error = 1.56% \u2248 1.6%.`},{id:"neet-2023-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Types of errors",q:"The errors in the measurement which arise due to unpredictable fluctuations in temperature and voltage supply are:",o:["Random errors","Instrumental errors","Personal errors","Least count errors"],c:0,s:`Errors that have no systematic or constant cause are random errors.
They arise from unpredictable changes in conditions, such as temperature, pressure or voltage supply.`},{id:"neet-2022-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions of angle",q:"Plane angle and solid angle have",o:["Dimensions but no units","No units and no dimensions","Both units and dimensions","Units but no dimensions"],c:3,s:`Plane angle = arc/radius = [L]/[L] = [M\u2070L\u2070T\u2070]; its unit is radian.
Solid angle = area/(radius)\xB2 = L\xB2/L\xB2 = [M\u2070L\u2070T\u2070]; its unit is steradian.
So both have units but no dimensions.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q028",pn:28,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional formula",q:"The physical quantity that has the same dimensional formula as pressure is:",o:["Coefficient of viscosity","Force","Momentum","Young's modulus of elasticity"],c:3,s:`Pressure = F/A, so [P] = MLT\u207B\xB2/L\xB2 = ML\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB2.
Young's modulus = stress/strain. Strain has no dimension, so [Y] = ML\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB2.
Thus Young's modulus has the same dimensions as pressure.`},{id:"neet-2022-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions of physical quantities",q:"The dimensions [MLT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB2] belong to the",o:["Self inductance","Magnetic permeability","Electric permittivity","Magnetic flux"],c:1,s:"The dimensional formula of magnetic permeability is [MLT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB2]."},{id:"neet-2022r-q037",pn:37,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Percentage error",q:"The percentage error in the measurement of g is: (Given that g = 4\u03C0\xB2L/T\xB2, L = (10 \xB1 0.1) cm, T = (100 \xB1 1)s)",o:["7%","2%","5%","3%"],c:3,s:`\u0394g/g \xD7 100 = \u0394L/L \xD7 100 + 2 \u0394T/T \xD7 100.
\u0394L/L \xD7 100 = 0.1/10 \xD7 100 = 1%.
2 \u0394T/T \xD7 100 = 2 \xD7 1/100 \xD7 100 = 2%.
Total = 1 + 2 = 3%.`},{id:"neet-2022-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Significant figures",q:"The area of a rectangular field (in m\xB2) of length 55.3 m and breadth 25 m after rounding off the value for correct significant digits is",o:["1382","1382.5","14 \xD7 10\xB2","138 \xD7 10\xB9"],c:2,s:`Area = 55.3 \xD7 25 = 1382.5 m\xB2.
Breadth 25 has two significant figures, so the answer keeps two.
Rounded value = 14 \xD7 10\xB2 m\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2021-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional formula",q:"If E and G respectively denote energy and gravitational constant, then E/G has the dimensions of",o:["[M\xB2][L\u207B\xB2][T\u207B\xB9]","[M\xB2][L\u207B\xB9][T\u2070]","[M][L\u207B\xB9][T\u207B\xB9]","[M][L\u2070][T\u2070]"],c:1,s:`[E] = [M L\xB2 T\u207B\xB2].
[G] = [M\u207B\xB9 L\xB3 T\u207B\xB2].
Divide: [M\xB9\u207B\u207D\u207B\xB9\u207E L\xB2\u207B\xB3 T\u207B\xB2\u207B\u207D\u207B\xB2\u207E] = [M\xB2 L\u207B\xB9 T\u2070].
So option (2).`},{id:"neet-2021-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Screw gauge",q:`A screw gauge gives the following readings when used to measure the diameter of a wire
Main scale reading : 0 mm Circular scale reading : 52 divisions
Given that 1 mm on main scale corresponds to 100 divisions on the circular scale. The diameter of the wire from the above data is`,o:["0.052 cm","0.52 cm","0.026 cm","0.26 cm"],c:0,s:`Pitch = 1 mm for 100 divisions.
Least count = 1/100 mm = 0.01 mm = 0.001 cm.
Diameter = 0 + 52 \xD7 0.001 cm = 0.052 cm.`},{id:"neet-2021-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional analysis",q:"If force [F], acceleration [A] and time [T] are chosen as the fundamental physical quantities. Find the dimensions of energy.",o:["[F][A\u207B\xB9][T]","[F][A][T]","[F][A][T\xB2]","[F][A][T\u207B\xB9]"],c:2,s:`Let E = F\u1D43 A\u1D47 T\u1D9C.
[E] = [M L\xB2 T\u207B\xB2], [F] = [M L T\u207B\xB2], [A] = [L T\u207B\xB2].
Right side = M^a L^(a+b) T^(\u20132a\u20132b+c).
Mass: a = 1.
Length: a + b = 2, so b = 1.
Time: \u20132a \u2013 2b + c = \u20132 gives \u20134 + c = \u20132, so c = 2.
E = [F][A][T\xB2].`},{id:"neet-2020-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Screw gauge",q:"A screw gauge has least count of 0.01 mm and there are 50 divisions in its circular scale. The pitch of the screw gauge is :",o:["0.25 mm","0.5 mm","1.0 mm","0.01 mm"],c:1,s:`Least count = pitch / number of circular divisions.
0.01 mm = pitch/50.
Pitch = 0.01 \xD7 50 = 0.5 mm.`},{id:"neet-2020-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Significant figures",q:"Taking into account of the significant figures, what is the value of 9.99 m \u2013 0.0099 m ?",o:["9.98 m","9.980 m","9.9 m","9.9801 m"],c:0,s:`9.99 \u2212 0.0099 = 9.9801.
In subtraction the result keeps as many decimal places as the term with fewer decimal places.
9.99 has 2 decimal places, so the answer is 9.98 m.`},{id:"neet-2020-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions",q:"Dimensions of stress are :",o:["[ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2]","[ML\u2070T\u207B\xB2]","[ML\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB2]","[MLT\u207B\xB2]"],c:2,s:`Stress = force/area.
Force = [MLT\u207B\xB2], area = [L\xB2].
Stress = [MLT\u207B\xB2]/[L\xB2] = [ML\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB2].`},{id:"neet-2020-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Unit conversion",q:"The energy required to break one bond in DNA is 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J. This value in eV is nearly :",o:["0.6","0.06","0.006","6"],c:1,s:`1 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
E = 10\u207B\xB2\u2070/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079) eV.
= 0.625 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 = 0.0625 eV \u2248 0.06 eV.`}]})();
